## 3. Worked example: one field, start to finish

Rather than reading every sheet in isolation, trace `TRADE_DATE` end-to-end. This is the pattern to replicate for any new field in a new config.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A["Raw column\nTRADE_DATE\n(SRC file, e.g. dd/mm/yy)"] -->|FieldMappings\nAction=AddColumn\nTransformFunction=ConvertDateFormat| B["TradeDate_SRC\n(normalized %Y-%m-%d)"]
    B --> C["NoOfWeekDaysBtwTradeDateAndPaymentDate_SRC\nFieldMappings, CalculateBusinessDays\ninputs: TradeDate_SRC, PaymentDate_SRC"]
    C --> D["IsNotShortDatedForward_CTRL\nEligibility rule\nuses day-count to exclude short-dated trades"]
    D --> E["IndepEligibility_CTRL\nEligibility rollup"]
    B --> F["TradeDate\nRecRules StandardField\nFormula: TradeDate_SRC if IsNotNull(TradeDate_SRC) else TradeDate_TGT"]
    F --> G["Final normalized record\n(used downstream in reporting/BI)"]
```

**Reading this pattern for a new field:**
1. Does the raw column need reformatting/renaming only? → FieldMappings row, `CopyField` or `ConvertDateFormat`, done.
2. Does another field depend on it (a calc, a flag)? → add a second FieldMappings row (or an Eligibility/Obligation rule) that lists it in `RuleInputs`/`TransformInputs`.
3. Does it exist on both SRC and TGT and need to appear once in the final record? → add a `StandardField` coalesce row in RecRules.
4. Only skip a step if the field genuinely has no downstream consumer — don't add rules "just in case."

