## 5. Field-level requirements: what every config must have vs what's asset-specific

**Minimum required field set (every control, every asset class) — generate these without asking:**

| Category | Fields |
|---|---|
| Identity | TradePartyTransactionId, UTI/USI equivalent, ReportingCounterpartyId, IdOfTheOtherCounterparty |
| Dates | TradeDate, EffectiveDate/StartDate, MaturityDate/ExpirationDate, PaymentDate (if applicable) |
| Economics | NotionalCurrency, NotionalAmount, BuySellFlag |
| Product | ProductType (raw + normalized ISDAPlusProductType equivalent) |
| Context (static per-row) | AssetClass, Regulation, Jurisdiction, SourceSystem, Region |
| Status | ActionType (new/amend/cancel), TradeStatus (live/matured/terminated) |

**Asset-class-additive fields (ask, or infer from the sample file's actual columns) — do not invent:**
- Anything specific to the product mechanics of that asset class (e.g. strike/premium for options, reset frequency for swaps, delivery point for commodities)
- Any field that only exists because of a regulator-specific reporting requirement for that asset class

**Rule of thumb:** if a column appears in the raw SRC/TGT sample file and maps cleanly to a minimum-required-field concept, map it automatically. If a column doesn't obviously map to anything in the minimum set, list it as "unmapped — needs classification" rather than guessing a purpose for it.

